1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,391
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,391 --> 00:00:08,218
This presentation examines how a language model can learn to improve an answer after it has already committed to one.

3
00:00:08,218 --> 00:00:15,654
The central paper is Training Language Models to Self-Correct via Reinforcement Learning, by Aviral Kumar, Vincent Zhuang, and colleagues.

4
00:00:15,654 --> 00:00:28,960
I will place its method, called score, in the wider literature on self-correction, explain its architecture, examine the benchmark comparisons and ablations, and discuss what an engineer should measure before using a revision loop.

5
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:31,308
This recording uses synthetic male narration.

6
00:00:31,308 --> 00:00:34,439
The research results belong to the cited authors.

7
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:39,917
The diagrams, worked examples, and engineering analysis are explanatory material created for this presentation.

8
00:00:41,603 --> 00:00:47,776
Consider a simple equation: three times x plus two, with parentheses around x plus two, equals fifteen.

9
00:00:47,776 --> 00:00:52,496
Dividing by three gives x plus two equals five, so x equals three.

10
00:00:52,496 --> 00:00:53,948
That answer is correct.

11
00:00:53,948 --> 00:01:00,847
Now imagine a revision that expands the left side incorrectly as three x plus two and returns thirteen thirds.

12
00:01:00,847 --> 00:01:04,841
The second response may sound careful while damaging a correct result.

13
00:01:04,841 --> 00:01:09,561
This is an original teaching example, not an output taken from the paper.

14
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:11,740
It captures the central evaluation problem.

15
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:17,550
We need to measure both the mistakes a model repairs and the correct answers it breaks.

16
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:23,359
A longer explanation, a confident critique, or a changed answer does not by itself establish improvement.

17
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:28,443
The relevant outcome is whether revision increases correctness across the same set of questions.

18
00:01:30,127 --> 00:01:32,136
Two surveys provide useful lenses.

19
00:01:32,136 --> 00:01:39,370
Pan and colleagues organize correction by timing: during training, during generation, and after an answer has been produced.

20
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:46,604
Kamoi and colleagues emphasize what information supports the feedback and whether evaluation actually tests the intended research question.

21
00:01:46,604 --> 00:01:49,418
I combine those lenses into three axes.

22
00:01:49,418 --> 00:01:51,025
The first is timing.

23
00:01:51,025 --> 00:01:59,465
The second is feedback: the model can criticize itself, receive tool or environment results, or learn from reference-based rewards during training.

24
00:01:59,465 --> 00:02:06,297
The third is adaptation: prompts can change, a text memory can grow, or model weights can change.

25
00:02:06,297 --> 00:02:07,503
These axes overlap.

26
00:02:07,503 --> 00:02:13,933
A post-hoc revision can use external tools, and a trained model can still perform post-hoc revision.

27
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:20,363
Treating the axes separately prevents the word reflection from hiding very different systems and experimental conditions.

28
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:28,578
Self-Refine uses one language model to produce an answer, offer feedback, and revise it without additional training.

29
00:02:28,578 --> 00:02:35,876
Reflexion adds a memory mechanism: an agent writes reflections from task feedback and uses those notes in later trials.

30
00:02:35,876 --> 00:02:40,102
Its verbal reinforcement does not mean gradient updates to model weights.

31
00:02:40,102 --> 00:02:45,096
Pair supervised fine-tuning learns from stored pairs of faulty attempts and repaired answers.

32
00:02:45,096 --> 00:02:49,706
A star style repair approach selects successful sampled trajectories for supervised learning.

33
00:02:49,706 --> 00:02:54,699
Score instead trains a policy on its own multi-turn trajectories using reinforcement learning.

34
00:02:54,699 --> 00:02:58,157
These descriptions explain the mechanisms, not a universal ranking.

35
00:02:58,157 --> 00:03:08,913
A tool-using agent with access to execution feedback is solving a different information problem from an intrinsic correction model that receives no new correctness signal at test time.

36
00:03:08,913 --> 00:03:12,754
The table makes those assumptions visible before any accuracy comparison.

37
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:18,580
A repair dataset can look successful while teaching the wrong behavior.

38
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:20,461
One issue is distribution mismatch.

39
00:03:20,461 --> 00:03:26,483
Suppose an older model produces short arithmetic errors, while a later model produces longer reasoning mistakes.

40
00:03:26,483 --> 00:03:31,375
Learning to repair the first distribution does not guarantee competence on the second.

41
00:03:31,375 --> 00:03:34,385
Another issue is collapse toward an easy strategy.

42
00:03:34,385 --> 00:03:41,159
If a training objective rewards final correctness, a policy may improve its initial answer and then repeat it.

43
00:03:41,159 --> 00:03:44,546
That can earn reward without learning much about revision.

44
00:03:44,546 --> 00:03:51,320
The engineering analogy is testing a repair service only on archived incidents that no longer resemble production failures.

45
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:57,718
The solution needs exposure to current failures and an objective that distinguishes useful correction from harmless repetition.

46
00:03:57,718 --> 00:04:05,620
These are explanations of the failure modes studied in the paper, rather than claims that every supervised repair method must fail.

47
00:04:07,306 --> 00:04:11,573
The architecture is a two-attempt interaction with the same trainable policy.

48
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:15,840
A question goes to the model, which generates its first solution.

49
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:24,373
The revision context then includes the original question, the earlier answer, and an instruction asking the model to check for possible error.

50
00:04:24,373 --> 00:04:29,416
During training, a verifier scores the responses using known answers or code tests.

51
00:04:29,416 --> 00:04:31,355
Those rewards update the policy.

52
00:04:31,355 --> 00:04:41,053
During intrinsic self-correction evaluation, the model does not receive the hidden answer, the reward, or test results telling it whether the first attempt was correct.

53
00:04:41,053 --> 00:04:42,604
This distinction is essential.

54
00:04:42,604 --> 00:04:45,320
Self-generated trajectories do not mean supervision-free learning.

55
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:51,526
The trajectories come from the model, while their training rewards still depend on verifiable task outcomes.

56
00:04:51,526 --> 00:04:58,508
The conceptual diagram separates that training-only scoring path from the inference path so the information boundary remains explicit.

57
00:05:00,194 --> 00:05:01,764
Score uses two stages.

58
00:05:01,764 --> 00:05:11,189
Stage one prepares an initialization that improves the second attempt while keeping the first-attempt distribution near the reference model through a stronger divergence constraint.

59
00:05:11,189 --> 00:05:17,865
The idea is to practice useful revision before the first attempt can absorb all the training progress.

60
00:05:17,865 --> 00:05:23,363
Stage two trains both attempts and adds a progress bonus to the second-turn reward.

61
00:05:23,363 --> 00:05:28,075
The bonus is alpha times the difference between second and first rewards.

62
00:05:28,075 --> 00:05:37,107
With binary correctness and an illustrative alpha of ten, changing a wrong answer to a correct one contributes a bonus of positive ten.

63
00:05:37,107 --> 00:05:39,856
Breaking a correct answer contributes negative ten.

64
00:05:39,856 --> 00:05:41,820
Unchanged correctness contributes zero bonus.

65
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:45,354
These are bonus values, not the complete training objective.

66
00:05:45,354 --> 00:05:49,281
The full method also includes correctness rewards and divergence regularization.

67
00:05:49,281 --> 00:05:52,815
This design makes recovery and preservation explicit training incentives.

68
00:05:54,500 --> 00:06:00,872
For mathematics, the authors fine-tune Gemini one point five Flash and evaluate on five hundred MATH problems.

69
00:06:00,872 --> 00:06:07,620
The training construction incorporates forty-five hundred problems from the original MATH test set, leaving five hundred for evaluation.

70
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:12,493
This split should be disclosed whenever these results are compared with other reports.

71
00:06:12,493 --> 00:06:18,491
For code, the authors fine-tune Gemini one point zero Pro on MBPP and evaluate on HumanEval.

72
00:06:18,491 --> 00:06:20,741
The main comparisons use greedy decoding.

73
00:06:20,741 --> 00:06:24,489
The model does not see HumanEval test cases during evaluation.

74
00:06:24,489 --> 00:06:30,487
This is a paper review: I have not retrained those proprietary models or rerun their benchmarks.

75
00:06:30,487 --> 00:06:37,235
The results shown next are transcribed from the paper, with source tables recorded in the accompanying data files.

76
00:06:37,235 --> 00:06:43,983
A reproducible analysis of published numbers is useful, but it should never be described as an independent replication.

77
00:06:45,667 --> 00:06:53,575
The mathematics comparison shows why we need to keep three quantities separate: first-attempt accuracy, second-attempt accuracy, and the change within each method.

78
00:06:53,575 --> 00:07:01,482
The base model moves from fifty-two point six percent to forty-one point four percent, a decline of eleven point two percentage points.

79
00:07:01,482 --> 00:07:07,592
Score moves from sixty percent to sixty-four point four percent, a gain of four point four points.

80
00:07:07,592 --> 00:07:12,624
Subtracting those gains gives an improvement of fifteen point six points in self-correction delta.

81
00:07:12,624 --> 00:07:16,577
The gap in final accuracy is a different number: twenty-three points.

82
00:07:16,577 --> 00:07:20,890
Pair supervised fine-tuning reaches fifty-four point two percent on its second attempt.

83
00:07:20,890 --> 00:07:25,922
These rows form a comparison table from this paper, not a current global leaderboard.

84
00:07:25,922 --> 00:07:28,798
They support a claim about the tested setup.

85
00:07:28,798 --> 00:07:34,548
They do not establish that the same ordering holds for every model, domain, or compute budget.

86
00:07:36,233 --> 00:07:38,893
The code results add an important qualification.

87
00:07:38,893 --> 00:07:44,592
Score begins at fifty-two point four percent and reaches sixty-four point six percent after revision.

88
00:07:44,592 --> 00:07:49,531
Its twelve point two point within-model gain exceeds the base model's three-point gain.

89
00:07:49,531 --> 00:07:54,470
But score's initial accuracy is below the base model's fifty-three point seven percent.

90
00:07:54,470 --> 00:07:58,269
Training for revision therefore does not automatically improve every measure.

91
00:07:58,269 --> 00:08:04,348
Pair supervised fine-tuning has a stronger initial score in this table, then loses accuracy after revision.

92
00:08:04,348 --> 00:08:12,706
An engineer should ask which operating point matters: one attempt under tight latency, or a trained two-attempt workflow with additional generation cost.

93
00:08:12,706 --> 00:08:15,366
Comparing only final accuracy hides that choice.

94
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:26,384
I also avoid combining the HumanEval numbers with unrelated headline scores from other papers, because different base models, feedback access, and retry budgets would make that combined ranking misleading.

95
00:08:28,068 --> 00:08:32,276
We can unpack the score mathematics row using the five-hundred-question denominator.

96
00:08:32,276 --> 00:08:38,015
Sixty percent initial accuracy means three hundred answers begin correct and two hundred begin wrong.

97
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:43,371
The reported recovery share of five point eight percent corresponds to twenty-nine repaired problems.

98
00:08:43,371 --> 00:08:48,345
The damage share of one point four percent corresponds to seven broken answers.

99
00:08:48,345 --> 00:08:53,318
The result is twenty-two additional correct answers, or four point four percentage points.

100
00:08:53,318 --> 00:08:55,231
Conditional rates answer different questions.

101
00:08:55,231 --> 00:09:00,588
Twenty-nine repairs out of two hundred initially wrong answers gives fourteen point five percent.

102
00:09:00,588 --> 00:09:06,327
Seven damages out of three hundred initially correct answers gives about two point three percent.

103
00:09:06,327 --> 00:09:13,596
The one point four percent in the table is a share of all problems, not the conditional damage rate.

104
00:09:13,596 --> 00:09:17,422
Recording denominators avoids a common error in discussing self-correction results.

105
00:09:19,106 --> 00:09:24,207
The ablation study removes components to test what they contribute in this setting.

106
00:09:24,207 --> 00:09:27,738
Full score gains four point four points between attempts.

107
00:09:27,738 --> 00:09:34,407
Removing multi-turn training changes that to a decline of two point four points, despite stronger first-attempt accuracy.

108
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:38,331
Removing stage one leaves a two point two point gain.

109
00:09:38,331 --> 00:09:41,469
Removing reward shaping leaves two point six points.

110
00:09:41,469 --> 00:09:47,354
Replacing second-stage reinforcement learning with star leaves two point two points and lowers final accuracy.

111
00:09:47,354 --> 00:09:52,847
These comparisons support the combination of multi-turn training, initialization, and reward shaping used here.

112
00:09:52,847 --> 00:09:56,378
They do not prove each component is universally necessary.

113
00:09:56,378 --> 00:10:00,694
The table also does not provide uncertainty intervals for these rows.

114
00:10:00,694 --> 00:10:07,363
I would want repeated seeds and paired uncertainty estimates before treating small differences as stable deployment advantages.

115
00:10:09,049 --> 00:10:13,278
A revision consumes computation that could have funded another independent sample.

116
00:10:13,278 --> 00:10:15,969
That makes the baseline an engineering decision.

117
00:10:15,969 --> 00:10:20,968
A useful study compares revision with repeated sampling or self-consistency under matched resources.

118
00:10:20,968 --> 00:10:24,428
The paper explores combining parallel sampling and sequential correction.

119
00:10:24,428 --> 00:10:31,348
However, a fixed number of solutions does not automatically guarantee equal token cost, wall-clock latency, or service expense.

120
00:10:31,348 --> 00:10:39,038
My proposed deployment evaluation would record every generated critique and revised answer, any verifier calls, and the final selection step.

121
00:10:39,038 --> 00:10:44,036
It would use the same questions and a predefined stopping rule across alternatives.

122
00:10:44,036 --> 00:10:49,803
Accuracy per dollar and accuracy at a latency target can change which design is preferable.

123
00:10:49,803 --> 00:10:59,414
A method that wins when allowed two full generations may be less attractive for a user who needs a reliable answer in one short interaction.

124
00:11:01,098 --> 00:11:03,048
Here is another original example.

125
00:11:03,048 --> 00:11:07,339
The question asks for the mean of ten, twenty, and sixty.

126
00:11:07,339 --> 00:11:09,289
A first attempt gives twenty-five.

127
00:11:09,289 --> 00:11:16,311
A useful revision identifies the relevant operation, computes the sum as ninety, counts three observations, and returns thirty.

128
00:11:16,311 --> 00:11:24,112
The important feature is a check tied to a specific failure, rather than generic reassurance that the answer was reconsidered.

129
00:11:24,112 --> 00:11:30,743
If the application permits a calculator or code execution, that tool can supply an independent arithmetic check.

130
00:11:30,743 --> 00:11:36,594
If it does not, the model must rely on its learned behavior and existing context.

131
00:11:36,594 --> 00:11:38,544
These are different evaluation settings.

132
00:11:38,544 --> 00:11:40,884
This example is deliberately labeled illustrative.

133
00:11:40,884 --> 00:11:49,076
It demonstrates what a successful correction would look like, but it is not evidence about score's performance on real unseen questions.

134
00:11:50,761 --> 00:11:57,102
My proposed LLM operations workflow starts with a versioned evaluation set and a clear information boundary.

135
00:11:57,102 --> 00:12:05,029
Each run records the model version, prompt version, verifier version, first answer, revised answer, correctness transition, token use, and latency.

136
00:12:05,029 --> 00:12:10,974
A dashboard should show final accuracy alongside recovery and damage, broken down by task family.

137
00:12:10,974 --> 00:12:16,918
Before a release, compare the revision policy against a no-revision baseline and an independent-sampling baseline.

138
00:12:16,918 --> 00:12:21,278
Use paired analysis because the same questions appear in each condition.

139
00:12:21,278 --> 00:12:24,845
Keep evaluation questions separate from tuning and release decisions.

140
00:12:24,845 --> 00:12:31,582
If code execution is used, sandbox it and log failures without exposing hidden tests to the model.

141
00:12:31,582 --> 00:12:36,734
This is an engineering proposal, not a deployed system included in the paper.

142
00:12:36,734 --> 00:12:41,490
Its purpose is to turn a research insight into measurable operational behavior.

143
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:47,694
The strongest aspect of this work is its evaluation of transitions.

144
00:12:47,694 --> 00:12:52,624
It asks whether a policy both fixes mistakes and protects correct answers.

145
00:12:52,624 --> 00:12:56,731
The ablations also connect observed behavior to specific training choices.

146
00:12:56,731 --> 00:12:58,785
My main reservation is scope.

147
00:12:58,785 --> 00:13:02,893
Binary rewards for mathematics and code provide relatively clean signals.

148
00:13:02,893 --> 00:13:09,054
An open-ended explanation can be partly correct, unsupported, outdated, or stylistically strong while still misleading.

149
00:13:09,054 --> 00:13:11,930
That makes verification and reward design harder.

150
00:13:11,930 --> 00:13:18,091
The experiments train over two attempts, so a longer autonomous loop introduces additional distribution changes.

151
00:13:18,091 --> 00:13:23,021
Proprietary base models also limit straightforward replication of the full training setup.

152
00:13:23,021 --> 00:13:35,755
I would treat score as evidence that a useful revision strategy can be learned under these conditions, then require application-specific evidence before relying on it for broader factual reliability or safety.

153
00:13:37,439 --> 00:13:42,437
The practical takeaway is to treat revision as a behavior with measurable benefits and costs.

154
00:13:42,437 --> 00:13:47,434
The survey taxonomy helps identify when correction happens, which feedback is available, and what adapts.

155
00:13:47,434 --> 00:13:56,097
The score study shows a concrete training design for intrinsic two-attempt correction and demonstrates why a strong initial answer alone does not settle whether revision helps.

156
00:13:56,097 --> 00:14:05,426
For a real application, I would begin with the simplest permitted baseline, measure recovery and damage, and add revision only when the benefit survives a fair cost comparison.

157
00:14:05,426 --> 00:14:10,091
I would also preserve the first answer in logs so that regressions remain visible.

158
00:14:10,091 --> 00:14:20,086
The central question is specific: on this workload, with this model and this budget, does another attempt produce enough additional correctness to justify the delay and the risk of damage?

159
00:14:21,772 --> 00:14:31,230
The accompanying article and repository contain the complete bibliography, clickable source links, benchmark data, original figures, presentation slides, and this narration transcript.

160
00:14:31,230 --> 00:14:37,678
The main source is Kumar and colleagues' score paper, version two from October twenty twenty-four.

161
00:14:37,678 --> 00:14:44,127
The surveys by Kamoi and colleagues and Pan and colleagues supply the broader conceptual context.

162
00:14:44,127 --> 00:14:47,136
Self-Refine and Reflexion provide representative contrasting techniques.

163
00:14:47,136 --> 00:14:51,435
All reported benchmark results are credited to the original paper.

164
00:14:51,435 --> 00:14:56,594
The accompanying calculation script checks the transcribed deltas and derived transition counts.

165
00:14:56,594 --> 00:14:58,314
Thank you for watching.
